Objective-C lexer incorrectly highlights Cocoa interfaces and protocols

#### Description

The Objective-C lexer is not properly highlighting Cocoa interfaces and protocols. Built-in Cocoa types like `NSString`, `NSArray`, and protocol names are not being recognized with the correct token type.

#### Steps/Code to Reproduce

```objective-c
#import <Foundation/Foundation.h>

@interface MyClass : NSObject
@property (nonatomic, strong) NSString *name;
@property (nonatomic, strong) NSArray *items;
@end

@protocol MyProtocol <NSObject>
- (void)doSomething;
@end
```

When highlighting this code, `NSObject`, `NSString`, `NSArray` and other Cocoa built-ins should be highlighted as built-in types, but they appear as regular names instead.

#### Expected Behavior

Cocoa interfaces like `NSString`, `NSArray`, `NSObject` and protocols should be highlighted with the `Name.Builtin` token type to distinguish them from user-defined classes and protocols.

#### Actual Behavior

These built-in Cocoa types are being highlighted as regular `Name` tokens, making them indistinguishable from user-defined identifiers in syntax highlighting.
